<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veracruz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Veracruz is a state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> stretching along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> coast between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. Its tropical lowlands were home to the Olmec centres of San Lorenzo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, the Classic city of El Tajín and the Huastec and Totonac peoples. The state takes its name from the port of Veracruz, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1519, and its capital is Xalapa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veracruz.jpg" alt="Veracruz" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Veracruz_in_Mexico_(location_map_scheme).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Veracruz, officially the Free and Sovereign State of Veracruz de Ignacio de la Llave, is one of the 31 states that with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> make up the 32 federal entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. It lies in eastern Mexico and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-luis-potosi">San Luis Potosí</a>, Hidalgo, Puebla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiapas">Chiapas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>. It is a crescent-shaped strip between the Sierra Madre Oriental and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, about 650 kilometres from north to south with an average width of about 100 kilometres, and covers 78,815 square kilometres. Its coastline runs for 690 kilometres, and the land rises from coastal plains to the Pico de Orizaba, at 5,636 metres Mexico's highest peak.</p><p>The state is divided into 212 municipalities. The suffix of its name honours Ignacio de la Llave y Segura Zevallos, governor in 1855 to 1857 and again in 1861 to 1863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Spanish arrival the region was populated mainly by four Indigenous groups: the Huastecs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomi">Otomis</a> in the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totonacs">Totonacs</a> in the centre-north and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmecs</a> in the south. Archaeological remains survive at Cempoala, El Tajín, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-lorenzo-tenochtitlan">San Lorenzo Tenochtitlán</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tres-zapotes">Tres Zapotes</a>, among other sites.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> and his expedition founded Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz on 18 May 1519, the first Spanish town in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. By doing so Cortés overthrew the authority of the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Diego Velázquez</a>, and claimed authority directly from the Spanish Crown. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-war-of-independence">Mexican War of Independence</a> much of the state backed the insurgents, who held most of it for the rest of the war, although the commercial class of the port did not support them.</p><p>In 1905 more than 5,000 workers of the El Valle Nacional company struck, and strikes at factories in Rio Blanco and Orizaba led to violence in January 1907. No major battles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> were fought in the state, but in 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a> temporarily moved the federal government there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Xalapa-Enríquez, usually called Xalapa. Other large cities include Veracruz, Boca del Río, Coatzacoalcos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, Minatitlán, Orizaba and Poza Rica. Its economy has long rested on agriculture and petroleum, and the Laguna Verde Nuclear Power Plant lies in Alto Lucero.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veracruz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Olmec heartland lies in the hot, wet lowlands along the southern Gulf of Mexico, in southern <b>Veracruz</b> and western Tabasco.</blockquote><a href="/culture/olmecs">Olmecs · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Many lived in the cities as servants, cooks, laundresses and skilled artisans, often hired out; others worked on the sugar estates of <b>Veracruz</b> and coastal Peru, in vineyards and in textile workshops (obrajes).</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;La Bamba&quot; is a Mexican folk song from the state of <b>Veracruz</b> and an example of son jarocho, a style of the region that combines Spanish (Andalusian), Indigenous, Afro-Mexican and Afro-Caribbean musical elements.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-bamba">La Bamba</a></li><li><blockquote>The last United States forces left the Mexican port city of <b>Veracruz</b>, which they had occupied since April in response to the Tampico Affair.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-23">23 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>El Tajín grew in the hills of north-central <b>Veracruz</b>, in a region of rich rainfall and tropical farming.</blockquote><a href="/state/el-tajin">El Tajín</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huastec separated from other Maya speakers well over two thousand years ago and settled the lowlands of northern <b>Veracruz</b>, San Luis Potosí and Tamaulipas.</blockquote><a href="/state/huastec-chiefdoms">Huastec chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
